# Assignment 1.1 — How a Website Works

A website opens on the screen through communication between the browser, client, server, and database. When a user types a URL (Uniform Resource Locator) into a browser such as Google Chrome, the browser acts as the client and sends an HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) or HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) request to the web server. HTTP is used to transfer data between the browser and server, while HTTPS provides a secure, encrypted connection. The server receives the request, processes it, and may communicate with a database to retrieve information such as user details, images, or other content. The server then sends an HTTP response containing the required HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, or other resources back to the browser. The browser processes these resources and displays the website on the screen. This process is called the request-response cycle. A static website displays fixed content that usually remains the same for every visitor, whereas a dynamic website can generate different content based on user input, database information, or other conditions. We can inspect this communication using Chrome DevTools by opening a website, pressing F12, selecting the Network tab, and refreshing the page to view requests, responses, status codes, and loading times.